WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:33.000
Vivek Bald: Our next presenter
is Elon Justice, whose thesis is

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.090
titled Hillbilly Talkback:
Co-Creation and

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:42.240
Counter-Narrative in Appalachia.
Kat Cizek, Eric, and I had have

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.990
had the honor and pleasure to
work with Elon. On her thesis

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:53.400
these last two years. Elon came
to CMS with a really inspiring

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.200
sense of clarity and purpose,
connected to her family roots in

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.190
Appalachia, her experiences in
media production and her

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:07.710
dedication to co creation as a,
an ethical mode of collective

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:12.780
self representation. Once she
started writing it, she made

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.950
things easy for Kat and me
because it just came, you know,

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:22.050
came forward, her writing just
beautifully written and

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:29.190
beautifully conceived. And I'm
just so excited to hear her

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.550
present and have her share this
work with all of you.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:33.540
Thanks.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.240
Elon Justice: I think you would
like me share my screen.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:52.560
Alright. So like you've heard
the title of my thesis is

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.530
Hillbilly Talkback: Co-Creation
and Counter-Narrative in

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.270
Appalachia. So I'd quickly just
like to give an overview of what

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.360
I want to talk about today. So
I'll start out with my research

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.480
questions. And a little bit of
background on why I actually

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.020
chose this topic. I'm then going
into introducing the problem

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.170
itself, which is the way that
Appalachia is represented in

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.490
popular media. Next, I'll go
into a quick breakdown of four

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.850
common stereotypes of Appalachia
that exist in popular media

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:25.770
today. And then I want to shift
into a look at my own

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.280
documentary project called the
Appalachian Retelling Project,

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.440
and looking at how it approaches
co creation in order to create

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.670
new narratives of Appalachia.
And then just finish it off with

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.160
some conclusions. And some
lessons learned from all of

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:48.390
this. So my three research
questions. The first is what

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.270
images have historically been
used to represent Appalachia in

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:55.590
popular media, and what do these
images mean? The second is how

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.350
my co creative documentary
practices work to produce new

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.410
counter narratives of the
region. And the final one is

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.380
what sorts of narratives emerge
from these documentaries that

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.350
use co creative methodologies.
So I'll be breaking down this

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.770
presentation into two halves.
The first half will focus on

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.320
this first question of how
Appalachia is currently

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.440
portrayed in popular media. And
the second half will focus on a

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.200
case study of my own project
that's attempting to challenge

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.360
these representations. I did
want to take a second to explain

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.030
why I chose this topic, because
it's actually a pretty personal

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.540
topic for me. And it's something
whose effects I started noticing

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.230
at a really young age. So I grew
up in a town called Pikeville,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:38.310
Kentucky, which you can see by
the green star on this map. I'm

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.670
located in far eastern Kentucky
close to both the West Virginia

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:45.480
and the Virginia borders. So
really in the heart of Central

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.980
Appalachia, which is the white
part on this map. And growing up

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.920
in the region, I think you tend
to become aware at a very young

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.940
age, that there's some sort of
difference between you and those

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.240
who live outside the mountains.
So I would notice it when my

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.820
parents and I went on vacation
that people might look at you

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:06.120
funny when they heard you talk
and they heard your accent. I

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.510
also remember watching TV, and I
would notice even as a kid that

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:12.600
Appalachia was pretty frequently
used either as the butt of a

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.510
joke, so maybe they would call
someone a hillbilly in order to

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.960
imply that they were dumb. And
other times I remember seeing it

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.650
portrayed as this object of
pity, just this place of utter

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.830
destitution, and helplessness.
And those were the only two

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:29.880
types of images that I ever saw
of my home. And neither of those

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.660
images captured the place that I
am. I'm so I'm going to talk

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.060
more about the broader effects
of this kind of representation

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.180
in a second. But there are a lot
of effects that I noticed from

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.810
this in my own life. I think
when you hear enough times that

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.600
you're not as smart or as
capable or as good as everyone

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.110
else, it's really hard to not
start believing it. So for

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.080
example, when I was 17, and I
started applying to colleges and

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.140
doing interviews, I remember
making this very conscious

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.600
effort to hide my accent,
because I didn't want the

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.910
interviewer to hear me say two
words and just decide that I was

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:03.480
Stupid. So I think that there's
this underlying sense that we're

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.480
less than or that were looked
down upon by others. And I think

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.180
that these sorts of effects tend
to trickle down and continue on

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.610
into new generations who feel
the same way. So I talked about

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.790
the effects that I noticed in my
own life. But as I began to do

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:20.550
more research on this topic, I
discovered that the effects were

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.250
actually much deeper and more
systemic than I had thought.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.270
Appalachia has been portrayed in
popular media through a fairly

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:31.020
narrow set of images for over
100 years. And all of these

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.930
images add up to create this
sense that Appalachia is this

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.040
other space. Historian Anthony
Anthony Harkins actually

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.030
describes agile Appalachia as a
place that is in but not of

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.440
America. Often this is done by
the media spending what might be

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.500
considered a positive cultural
trait. So something like having

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.650
a tight knit family structure
into something negative, like

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:56.970
incest. I think doing so makes
it much easier for governments,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.180
organizations, and just the
public at large to ignore the

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.020
problems that Appalachia faces.
And so issues like a higher than

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.450
average poverty rate, which is
exacerbated by things like

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.900
periodic flooding, lack of
broadband access, and inadequate

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.780
infrastructure, keeping people
trapped in the cycle of poverty,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.630
as well as the region's opioid
epidemic continues to go

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:16.140
unsolved.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.560
Because of the effects that I
just mentioned, and an overall

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.530
lack of job opportunity in
Appalachia, the region in the

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.320
last 10 years especially has
seen a large portion of its

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:29.770
population move out of the
region. This phenomenon has been

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:33.010
called a brain drain because
many of those who are left who

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:36.460
have left are young people at
the college degree or higher.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.610
But on the other hand, those who
continue to live in the region

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.280
are far more likely to struggle
with and die from what's been

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:46.000
called diseases of despair,
including things like alcohol

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.330
abuse, prescription and illegal
drug overdose and suicide

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:53.440
compared to people who live
elsewhere. Another effect of the

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.110
way that Appalachia is
represented is that Appalachia

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:00.340
has become very acquainted with
whiteness. Although nearly 20%

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.610
of Appalachia population is non
white, and the area has become

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:07.390
increasingly diverse since 2010.
The region continues to be

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:11.140
portrayed almost entirely
through white people. And what's

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.420
interesting though, is that
author Meredith McCarroll

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:16.600
actually argues that popular
media often portrays Appalachian

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:19.480
people using the same tropes
that are used to represent

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:23.290
people, people of color. She
says this marks Appalachian

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.710
people as phenotypically white,
yet hierarchically non white,

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:30.250
and she turns this
categorization un-white. I do

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.920
want to emphasize here that by
this term, I'm not trying to say

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:35.650
that white Appalachian people
face the same legal or systemic

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.770
oppression faced by people of
color, or the Appalachians are

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:42.130
in effect, some sort of racial
minority. Instead, I mean, to

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:44.410
call attention to the way that
the subordination of a

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:48.760
phenotypically white Appalachia
to a normal America both upholds

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.150
and privileges some sort of true
whiteness, while simultaneously

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:56.320
erasing the existence of people
of color in Appalachia. And

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.810
finally, the way that Appalachia
is represented has effects on

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.480
the identity and self worth of
those who live there. These were

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.960
the effects that I noticed most
in my own life. One study

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.810
actually found that Appalachian
representation has caused many

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:10.870
in the region to form identities
based upon the US then

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:14.980
mentality, which prevents people
from uniting within and between

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:18.430
identity groups. Also, more
anecdotally, many Appalachian

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:21.040
people speak about feeling out
of place and institutions of

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.680
power, or just outside the
region in general, which is

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:26.020
something I have definitely
personally experienced during my

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:32.140
time at MIT. So I've discussed
the effects, but how can we be

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:35.260
sure that media representation
is actually one of the causes of

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:39.190
what's going on in Appalachia?
I'm framing my argument through

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:42.580
the work of Stuart Hall and
cultural studies, Hall writes,

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.310
the events have no meaning until
they're represented, whether

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:48.040
that's in language, visual
communication, or some other

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:52.180
type of representation. Because
of this representation is deeply

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.600
intertwined with questions of
power. Paul asks the question,

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:58.780
Who has the power in what
channels to circulate which

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.690
meanings to whom to call
attention to this relationship

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:05.740
between representation and
power, also writes that meaning

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.650
is a very fluid thing, and that
it can never be finally fixed.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:11.500
However, certain meanings do
become privileged at different

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.010
points in time through systems
of ideology, which Hall defines

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.890
as the frameworks of thinking
and calculation about the world,

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.710
the ideas with which people
figure out how the social world

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:23.470
works, what their places in it,
and what they ought to do.

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:26.320
dominant ideologies are
perpetuated through

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:29.410
representation, and especially
media representation, because

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.810
it's the principal means through
which meanings are distributed

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:35.890
in our society today. Media
representation is especially

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.500
important in shaping how we
think about other groups of

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:41.320
people, because a lot of times
this is the only way that we

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:44.590
actually experienced these other
groups. Hall writes about the

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:47.620
way that difference is used as a
signifier in media and the

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:50.320
images of difference are often
read in broader terms of

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.620
cultural belonging. Generally,
the underlying message behind

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.350
these images is that those who
are some way different don't

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:04.520
So Next I'd like to just give a
brief breakdown of four common

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:56.800
belong

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.250
tropes that I've identified that
are used to portray Appalachia

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:11.630
in popular media. I've named
these degraded, violent,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.800
placeless, and white. I broken
each of these image types down

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.010
into subcategories, which you
can see below the headings. And

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:21.650
in my thesis, I lay out the
history of each image type, but

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.200
for now, I will just leave it by
saying that each of these tropes

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:27.140
has a very long history and
representations of Appalachia.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.440
So for example, images of
degraded Appalachia can actually

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:34.220
be traced back as far as the
1700s. And then off to the side,

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.790
I've given an example of either
a modern are a popular example

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:41.930
of each trope. So for degraded,
we've got Hillbilly Elegy, which

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:45.320
is a book that was a New York
Times bestseller in both 2016

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:48.950
and 2017. And was recently
turned to a film directed by Ron

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:52.580
Howard. For violence,
Deliverance, which has remained

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:55.490
a very popular and well known
film, despite being released

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:59.180
nearly 50 years ago. For place
lessness, The Beverly

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:02.570
Hillbillies, which was a hugely
popular sitcom in the 60s, and

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:05.270
I'm pretty sure still holds the
record for being the highest

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:10.040
rated rated half hour episode of
television in history. And for

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:12.560
whiteness, which I actually want
to explain that I chose this,

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:15.260
for lack of a better term,
because I'm trying to encompass

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.650
these three ideas of being white
male dominated, and this kind of

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:22.580
dangerous or extremist brand of
Christianity. But I chose The

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:25.370
Devil All the Time, which
actually came out on Netflix

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:28.910
last year, and it stars Tom
Holland, Bill Skarsgård, Robert

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:35.180
Pattinson, just this very well
known and popular cast. Well, I

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:38.210
don't have time to show an
example of all the images that I

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:41.000
talked about in my thesis, I did
want to play at least one clip

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:43.130
to give you an idea of the kinds
of images that I'm talking

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:46.820
about. So this is an ABC special
hosted by Diane Sawyer that

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:50.930
aired in 2009. I was in eighth
grade when a special came out.

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:53.480
And I think it had a bit big
impact on a lot of people my

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.810
age. And I'm going to play the
first two or so minutes and you

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:59.870
can see a lot of the imagery
that is very common in

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:03.590
representing Appalachia. Let me
know if you can't hear this.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:10.080
TV Voiceover: Tonight on 2020,

00:12:10.589 --> 00:12:12.419
Diane Sawyer special.

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:19.530
Singer: In the deep, dark hills
of eastern Kentucky

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:21.600
Diane Sawyer: Travel inside a
world apart.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:27.720
Singer: That's the place where I
traced my bloodline

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:29.880
Diane Sawyer: To the creeks and
steep hollers of the oldest

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:33.630
mountains in America, home to
the descendants of pioneers,

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:37.500
driven by their dreams to call
through these dangerous passes

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:41.490
and create an American
continent. They are brave,

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:45.390
tough, wary of outsiders steeped
in family and faith.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:48.690
They are also America's
legendary fighters.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:51.270
Interviewee: On a portionate
basis, the Appalachian

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:55.890
population has lost more men and
women in America's wars than any

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:57.780
other part of the American
nation.

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:01.050
Diane Sawyer: So how is it so
many of these people have been

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:04.950
left behind? We take you tonight
to one of the poorest regions in

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:08.100
the nation, where men and women
die younger than other

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:11.340
Americans, where there's a
documented epidemic of drug

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.370
addiction, cancer,
toothlessness, alcoholism,

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:15.270
depression.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:24.180
Singer: When the sun goes up
about ten in the morning and the

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:25.440
sun goes down

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:30.660
about three in the day

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:37.560
Elon Justice: Um, so now that
we've looked at some of the

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:40.170
common harmful ways that
Appalachia is represented in

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.840
media, I want to turn to the
second half of my research,

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:46.050
which focuses on how to create
new representations of

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:49.800
Appalachia that challenge these
old stereotypes. So first, I'd

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.890
quickly like to return to Stuart
Hall's work in representation.

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:55.740
Like I mentioned before, he
writes that the meaning of an

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:58.920
image can never be finally
fixed. And he uses the phrase

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:01.680
politics of the image to
describe this ongoing struggle

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:05.040
for any particular meaning to be
dominant. Because meaning can

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:08.130
never be finally fixed. This
opens up space to create counter

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:10.650
representations, which challenge
the current regime of

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:14.910
representation around a certain
topic or group. However, three

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:17.310
potential strategies to
challenge a dominant dominant

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:20.130
regime of representation, but
for the sake of time, I'll just

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:23.940
talk about the third one, which
he refers to as going inside the

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:26.610
image. This means that you
actually focus on the

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:29.730
stereotypes themselves that are
used to portray a group and you

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:32.400
try to complicate what that
stereotype means and how it came

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:35.910
to exist in the first place.
Hall says that this third

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:39.180
strategy is the most effective
not only at creating new images

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.640
of a group, but it's actually
challenging the way that they're

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:48.510
represented altogether. But in
addition to studying counter

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:51.780
narratives, I'm also really
interested in how co creation

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:54.900
and co creative documentary
specifically can work to produce

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:58.320
these counter narratives. To
give a sort of broad definition

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:01.650
of what co creation is. William
Uricchio and Kat Cizek define it

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:04.680
as an alternative to the single
authorship model that's common

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:08.160
in media production, and refers
to a constellation of frameworks

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.920
and production methods rather
than one specific methodology.

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:14.970
cocreation also allows projects
to emerge from within

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:19.320
communities and often involves a
wide variety of perspectives. It

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.230
requires deep listening on the
part of everyone involved. And

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.960
usually the final project isn't
defined at the outset, but

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:29.880
rather in excuse me, evolves out
of this process of listening and

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:34.440
discovery. Co-creation also seek
to challenge the power dynamics

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:37.290
that come with traditional modes
of media production, to Cizek

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:40.650
and Uricchio write that co
creation seeks to decolonize the

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:43.470
systems that oppressed and
stimulate projects that don't

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:46.590
simply document or passively
observe the world but insist on

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:50.430
change. So for these reasons, I
was really interested in seeing

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:52.980
how this type of methodology
that seeks to level power

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:56.220
imbalances could work to produce
new narratives of Appalachia

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:58.500
that would challenge the way
that it's currently represented.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:04.440
Although in my thesis, I look at
two case studies of co-creative

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:07.050
documentaries set in Appalachia.
For the sake of time, I just

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:11.400
want to talk about one which is
my own project. I started the

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:15.510
Appalachian Retelling Project in
July of 2020. And it's a web

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:18.870
based participatory documentary
that aims to challenge

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.630
stereotypes of Appalachia
through collective storytelling.

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:25.710
So participants can submit
written video, photo, audio or

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:29.010
other content to me on nearly
any topic related to life in

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:32.700
Appalachia that I will then post
on the website, I provide

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.220
prompts online to get people to
think about stories from their

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:38.310
own lives, or the lives of their
family and community that might

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:41.490
act as a counter narrative of
Appalachia. And although these

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:44.490
prompts cover several different
subject areas, they all tie back

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:47.370
to a general theme, which is
what is the story about

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:53.490
Appalachia that you haven't seen
in mainstream media. I just

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:56.130
started off the project. And to
give people an idea of the types

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:59.490
of stories that I was looking
for, I recorded five short form

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:02.100
documentary videos that I
released on the website

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:05.310
throughout the last week of
July. Each video featured a

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:08.190
different Appalachian person who
spoke about a different topic

00:17:08.340 --> 00:17:11.310
that was supposed to act as a
counter narrative. So I want to

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:14.250
show this video now, which is
actually a trailer that I made

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:17.730
for the project to try to get
more people to submit. But it

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:20.820
also features some clips from my
initial interviews, and also

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:23.280
some of the stories that were
submitted, just so you can get a

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:24.840
kind of general sense of the
project.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.360
Unknown: I had a story I wanted
to tell.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:39.240
Oh, yes, I want to tell a story
that defines what Appalachia is.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:41.280
And I'd like to tell that

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.110
There's kind of this analogy
that I like to use. There's like

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:49.980
these two mountains. And one
mountain is the official

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.050
narrative. And this other
mountain you have is the

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:55.620
vernacular history is the oral
history. And in the middle, like

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:58.050
here in Appalachia, that's
that's where the hollers that

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:01.800
are the two mountains meet. And
that's also where you find the

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:03.690
most bio-diversity is in the
holler

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:06.810
if we want to paint Eastern
Kentucky, so it's like, whatever

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:09.990
the media does, you know,
uneducated or whatever kind of

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:13.350
people. I don't think that it's
I don't think that it's fair to

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.470
do that, because I think 99% of
people

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:18.210
are really, really great.

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:29.760
I have seen a lot in media, of
how Appalachia is portrayed, and

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.640
it's wrong. It's not what we
are. It's not

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:34.650
how we live,

00:18:34.919 --> 00:18:40.049
telling the story of a white
male place filled with lazy

00:18:40.049 --> 00:18:43.079
white males, impacts

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:45.570
policy.

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:49.350
It erases women it erases people
of color.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:39.990
think it's important to
reimagine Appalachia, right?

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:44.730
We've had one narrative for a
long time. And honestly, it's

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:48.900
sort of like cheeps, the region
of all of its complexities,

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:53.790
right, all of the nuances like
like, we don't have to be just

00:19:53.790 --> 00:19:54.420
one thing.

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:57.360
Interviewee: There's so many
people with amazing stories

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:02.490
about resilience and stories
about What the region does well

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:06.660
without romanticizing it,
without painting it as a place

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:11.940
that has given up. I think it
would paint a truer picture of

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:13.620
the region and a picture that
would

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:15.390
Unknown: actually help.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:27.870
Elon Justice: So some aspects of
this project that we're co

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:31.920
creative, I'm actually dividing
my approach into two categories.

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:35.670
And the first is co creation
between myself as a media maker

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:39.210
with the Appalachian community.
And the second is co creation as

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.180
a member of the Appalachian
community myself, because I do

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:44.760
think that being Appalachian
affected how I approached this

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:47.280
project in ways that it might
not have if I had been from

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:51.540
somewhere else. So to summarize
my approach to co creation with

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:54.870
the community, I began asking
for feedback from people in the

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:58.200
community very early on as I
started this project, my

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:00.960
original idea was that the only
way to participate would be

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:03.870
through video. So kind of
continuing with the trend of the

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:07.350
original videos that I made. But
I got a lot of feedback that

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.390
most people wouldn't be
comfortable either with speaking

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:12.960
on camera, or doing these like
selfie style videos, or just

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:16.440
making their own videos in
general. So I started to adjust

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:19.770
my approach to allow people to
contribute in whichever way they

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:22.950
felt most comfortable. And I
eventually decided to just open

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:25.710
it up to basically any media
format. So including written

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:30.270
stories, photos, and videos. The
submissions process is

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:32.610
definitely the most
participatory aspect of this

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:36.300
project. I opened the project to
submissions the first week of

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.810
august 2020. And it's been open
ever since. So like I said,

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:43.140
people can submit in any media
format that they want. But they

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:45.960
can also speak about pretty much
any topic that they want, as

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.390
long as it's related to
Appalachia in some way. And it

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:52.110
doesn't violate our community
standards. I provide a list of

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:54.480
prompts on the website to get
people thinking about potential

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:58.530
topics to write about. So a
couple of examples are how have

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:01.530
you or someone you know, found
fulfillment in this region when

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:04.200
the media tells us that in order
to be successful, we have to

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:07.980
leave? And what's an example of
a portrayal of Appalachia and

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:10.350
media? They got it wrong, what
was wrong about it? And how

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:13.470
could it be better? These
questions are supposed to get

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.230
people to think about Appalachia
from the perspective of counter

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:20.190
narrative, and to directly speak
to exist existing stereotypes of

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.860
the region. However,
participants don't have to hold

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:25.440
to these questions. And I've
gotten submissions covering

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:28.650
everything from family histories
to like funny stories that have

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:31.980
been handed down between
generations. And I wouldn't say

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:34.800
that these sorts of stories
necessarily function as counter

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:38.310
narratives by themselves. But I
did decide to include them.

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:41.040
Because I think that just having
more media out there in the

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:43.620
world that's made by
Appalachians and allows them to

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:46.620
speak for themselves is still
something good and a step in the

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:51.540
right direction. In November of
2020, I decided to introduce

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.760
another way for people to
participate, which I've called

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:57.720
the Appalachian talkback series.
Basically, what I do is every

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:00.510
Monday, I post a question on
social media. And you can see an

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:03.630
example here on the left, that I
would then ask people to answer

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.910
in the comments. And I've
covered a lot of different

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:09.750
topics in the series, like media
representation, and the effects

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.450
of stereotypes. So things like
feeling pressured to code

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:16.740
switch. And I would say that
this, this series has actually

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:19.050
been one of the more successful
things that I've done in the

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:22.710
project, because more people
tend to participate because the

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:25.170
barrier to entry is much lower,
because you're just commenting

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:28.530
on a post rather than making a
whole submission. And it's also

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:31.110
much more pointed toward
creating counter neck counter

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:34.440
narratives and speaking directly
to stereotypes, rather than just

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.080
hoping that people might sit
submit that sort of content on

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:41.280
their own. I'd also like to
quickly talk about how I

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:43.710
approached co creation as a
member of the community in

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:47.310
Appalachia, I think the biggest
impact that being Appalachian

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:50.100
had on the way that I approached
this project is that it was a

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:53.310
huge influence on my decision to
be as flexible and as hands off

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:56.760
as possible in the type of
content that I received, both

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:59.340
through doing research for my
thesis, and also just growing up

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:02.250
noticing the way that Appalachia
was portrayed in media, I

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:05.520
realized that the vast majority
of popular media on Appalachia

00:24:05.550 --> 00:24:09.090
is created by is created by
people who didn't grow up there,

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:12.330
who didn't really spend an
extended amount of time there

00:24:12.330 --> 00:24:14.610
other than the time that it took
to make their news report their

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:17.910
film, and who really didn't seem
to understand the impact that

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:21.480
they were having on people that
live there. I also wasn't aware

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:23.790
of many opportunities for
Appalachian people to speak for

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:26.730
themselves in any sort of media,
no matter who had created it,

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:30.510
without heavy involvement on the
part of a director and editor.

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:33.900
So since COVID, had made it so I
would have to do this project

00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:37.260
online. Anyway, I wanted to use
the affordances of the web to

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:39.990
give as many people a chance to
talk as possible. And I didn't

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:42.720
want to get in the way of what
they were saying. So that's

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:45.600
ultimately why I decided to open
up the project to any kind of

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:48.660
topic, even if it wasn't exactly
the kind of story that I was

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.780
most interested in. And it's
also why I only edited

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:54.270
submissions for spelling and
grammar. And I didn't make any

00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:57.150
other major changes because I
really wanted each person to

00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:00.420
retain their own voice and their
own agency and the way They

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:01.530
wanted to tell their story.

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:07.180
Also, being from Appalachia
allowed me to tap into pre

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:10.270
existing, pre existing networks
that I had, when it came to

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:13.750
things like borrowing equipment
to make videos, and finding the

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:17.230
people to participate in my
initial five videos, actually,

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:20.800
of the people who participated
have the five of them, I already

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:24.010
knew three of them personally.
The fourth was someone that my

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:26.650
mom suggested that I should talk
to. And the fifth was just

00:25:26.650 --> 00:25:28.840
someone I kind of reached out
to, and they agreed to do it.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:32.230
But I think having this network
already in place was really

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:35.740
helpful, both in getting people
to participate, but also in

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:38.350
already knowing a lot about
these people's stories. So I

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:41.290
think that helped me ask better
questions, and really get to the

00:25:41.290 --> 00:25:44.950
heart of the issues that I
wanted to talk about. And

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:48.550
finally, being from Appalachia
was really helpful in growing my

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:52.210
audience. And I was also really
blown away by the response that

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:55.720
I got in the community, I mainly
promoted my work through social

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:58.450
media, and people were really
excited about this work, and

00:25:58.450 --> 00:26:00.940
they were really willing to
share it. And they would also

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:03.760
suggest people that I should
reach out to and offer to help

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:06.670
in other ways. And I really
don't think that I would have

00:26:06.670 --> 00:26:09.430
gotten this response if I had
been from somewhere else was and

00:26:09.430 --> 00:26:13.420
was doing the same work. Because
there is definitely a distrust

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:17.200
of outsiders in Appalachia. So I
think people would have probably

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:20.500
been wary of what my real
intentions were. But on the flip

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:23.200
side, there's also a very strong
culture of support within

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:25.840
Appalachia. So there's this
sense that if one of us

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:28.900
succeeds, that we all succeed.
And I think that's also a big

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:31.360
reason that people supported the
project like they did, because

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:37.540
it felt like a win for all of
us. And now I'd like to move on

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:39.940
to some of the counter
narratives that have come out of

00:26:39.940 --> 00:26:43.750
the project. The first one, I
think the most common narrative

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:47.680
is a celebration and a pride and
Appalachian culture, history and

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:51.820
people. This is counter to a lot
of popular media representations

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:54.820
that tend to portray Appalachia
as this place to be ashamed of

00:26:54.820 --> 00:26:57.910
or a place that needs to be
escaped. But instead, people

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:00.490
will talk about their love and
their connection to Appalachia

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:03.190
and the people who lived there,
even if they did acknowledge

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:07.300
that it had problems. The second
counter narrative was

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:10.390
highlighting the diversity of
Appalachia, whether that's

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:12.880
racial and ethnic diversity,
religious diversity, or

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:16.540
highlighting the experiences of
women. This is counter to most

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:19.300
popular portrayals of the region
that show it as this completely

00:27:19.300 --> 00:27:23.530
white male dominated and
Christian place. And the final

00:27:23.530 --> 00:27:26.890
count counter narrative is a
direct speaking back or a

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:30.670
critique of Appalachian media
representation and stereotypes

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:34.000
in general. And a lot of people
actually went on beyond just

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.280
calling these stereotypes out,
and they would go on to talk

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:38.860
about the effects of stereotypes
in their own lives and in their

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:44.860
communities. So I'd like to
finish with just a couple of

00:27:44.860 --> 00:27:48.100
conclusions from all of this.
The first is the importance of

00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:51.040
thinking about director versus
participant control in

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:54.490
co-creative projects. And the
way that prioritizing one over

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:57.910
the other can affect the
outcome. I decided up front to

00:27:57.910 --> 00:28:00.460
make the Appalachian retailing
project extremely purchase

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:03.910
participant driven. So my
strongest involvement was really

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:06.460
at the beginning in making the
first five videos and and

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:09.160
creating prompts and and
continuing to engage with people

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:12.880
on social media. But otherwise,
I tried to let people use this

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:15.670
project as a platform to say
whatever it is that they want to

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:19.540
say, and just for me to be the
person who promotes it. And I've

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:22.540
learned that this approach has
both affordances and drawbacks.

00:28:22.810 --> 00:28:25.690
So some of the drawbacks are
that since this project is so

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:28.810
open ended, a lot of the content
that's on the website doesn't

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:31.900
necessarily achieve my goal of
creating counter narratives, at

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:36.430
least by itself. A lot of people
are just sending me like family

00:28:36.430 --> 00:28:38.920
histories are finding stories.
And they're not thinking more

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:41.470
deeply about whether or not
they're trying to undermine

00:28:41.470 --> 00:28:44.500
stereotypes of Appalachia. And I
think if I had been more

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:47.380
involved in the process of them
creating, I could have worked

00:28:47.380 --> 00:28:49.420
more with them to make them
think about the topic in that

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:53.200
way. But on the other hand, I've
also received a lot of feedback

00:28:53.230 --> 00:28:56.290
that giving people this platform
has been extremely empowering,

00:28:56.500 --> 00:28:59.800
