process. The next part is it's a

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:33.940
common response. So what is the
response for you as a director?

00:44:33.940 --> 00:44:38.980
How, how are you going to move
forward in continuing to open up

00:44:38.980 --> 00:44:42.880
that space into a third space
and use space between those two

00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:44.290
very locked binaries?

00:44:44.890 --> 00:44:47.050
Elon Justice: Yeah, um, so
that's something I've kind of

00:44:47.050 --> 00:44:49.420
been thinking a lot about
lately, especially just in

00:44:49.420 --> 00:44:54.130
regards to the project is like
what is next? Because I feel

00:44:54.130 --> 00:44:57.790
like now could be a fitting time
to end it, but I also feel like

00:44:57.790 --> 00:45:02.380
there's a lot more that could be
done in this space. So I think

00:45:02.380 --> 00:45:05.350
personally, what I would do with
the project is I would love to

00:45:05.350 --> 00:45:10.090
start actually working more with
people on how to think about

00:45:10.090 --> 00:45:13.570
their stories. I think that I
got some great stuff just by

00:45:13.570 --> 00:45:16.360
being very open about it. And I
think I would still allow that.

00:45:17.140 --> 00:45:20.530
But I would love to, like, just
get to learn more about like

00:45:20.530 --> 00:45:23.050
what people are doing and what
people are talking about, and

00:45:23.230 --> 00:45:25.810
just reach out to them and say,
Hey, would you like to work with

00:45:25.810 --> 00:45:29.260
me? And we can think about how
to tell the story together? I

00:45:29.260 --> 00:45:32.200
think that, you know, it's like,
we've talked about how like,

00:45:32.320 --> 00:45:35.710
it's not really a binary between
participant control and director

00:45:35.710 --> 00:45:38.020
control. It's really a spectrum.
And you need to think about

00:45:38.020 --> 00:45:41.560
where you fit on that spectrum.
And what's the most beneficial

00:45:41.560 --> 00:45:44.830
spot to be? And so I think
that's, that's kind of what I'm

00:45:44.830 --> 00:45:47.830
figuring out right now is where
should I be on the spectrum? How

00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:50.200
much should I work with people?
How much should I give them

00:45:50.200 --> 00:45:51.790
space to say what they want to
say?

00:45:52.570 --> 00:45:52.990
Unknown: So yeah.

00:45:57.340 --> 00:46:00.760
Vivek Bald: There's one question
actually quite, quite related to

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:06.970
this in the in the q&amp;a. And that
is from Joshua Glick. And that

00:46:06.970 --> 00:46:09.610
is fascinating presentation. I
love all the collaborative

00:46:09.610 --> 00:46:12.700
dimensions of the project. Are
you thinking of synthesizing

00:46:12.700 --> 00:46:16.090
some of the research and aspects
of the larger project into a

00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:20.950
long form documentary? Or are
you leaning towards the

00:46:21.250 --> 00:46:24.190
co-creative process continuing
to co creative process?

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:27.340
Elon Justice: I think I lean
more toward the co-creative

00:46:28.090 --> 00:46:32.440
process. I think that making
long form films has just never

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:35.560
been my area of interest. For
one, I have a very short

00:46:35.560 --> 00:46:38.920
attention span. I think that's
why I gravitate toward short

00:46:38.920 --> 00:46:43.780
form content. But I also think
that it's just like, I don't

00:46:43.780 --> 00:46:49.330
want this to be some final, like
art. Like thing. I can't think

00:46:49.330 --> 00:46:51.400
of the word. I don't want it to
be some final things that just

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:54.910
never changes. I feel like this
is something that can keep going

00:46:54.910 --> 00:46:58.210
on. And that can keep expanding.
So I think that's what I'm

00:46:58.210 --> 00:46:58.900
thinking about.

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:04.140
Vivek Bald: All right. Thanks so
much, Elon.

